/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md | sed -n '1,380p'" in /Users/user/code-projects/chief-command
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chief Command — Architecture of Record (2026-07-14)
     2	
     3	Written after five adversarial cross-family reviews — 9 flaws, then 18, then a hollow
     4	core, then 3 verified schema holes, then the deepest finding of all: **an agent can get
     5	harmful code deployed by passing every gate honestly, no forgery required.** This is the
     6	corrected design. **Almost nothing here is built** — this is the spec Sol must approve
     7	before the rest is written.
     8	
     9	Plain English throughout, because the owner cannot read code.
    10	
    11	Three questions in this document were marked **OWNER DECISION** — they reversed or
    12	constrained directions Neill personally set, and Sol made them conditions of approval.
    13	**Neill decided all three on 2026-07-15.** The decisions are recorded inline where they
    14	apply and collected at the end.
    15	
    16	---
    17	
    18	## The one-sentence shape
    19	
    20	**OpenClaw drives the car. A tiny release service holds the only keys. Everything else —
    21	including the code the agents write — is treated as untrusted until a narrow, contained
    22	release path lets exactly one reviewed package through.**
    23	
    24	---
    25	
    26	## The core claim, stated honestly
    27	
    28	The previous version claimed *"a missed checkpoint is harmless."* **That is false, and
    29	the reason is the most important sentence in this document:**
    30	
    31	> Model review is a QUALITY gate, not a containment boundary. An agent — fooled by prompt
    32	> injection, or simply subtle — can put a harmful payload in its branch, the model
    33	> reviewers can inspect it and honestly pass it, the tester can screenshot the happy path,
    34	> the database can honestly record real passes, and the release service can deploy it.
    35	> Then the deployed code holds the production authority the agent never had.
    36	
    37	Branch protection controls *who merges*. It does not prove merged code is safe. A
    38	screenshot proves one visible flow worked. It does not prove there is no hidden payload.
    39	
    40	So the claim this design actually defends is the weaker, true one:
    41	
    42	> **A skipped checkpoint cannot directly obtain release authority. Production containment,
    43	> independent review, and narrow deployment privileges limit the harm of code that passes
    44	> review.**
    45	
    46	Everything below serves that claim. Where a sentence promises a guarantee, it names the
    47	real boundary that provides it — and calls out where the honest answer is "this reduces
    48	harm" rather than "this prevents it."
    49	
    50	---
    51	
    52	## The threat model
    53	
    54	Not the owner. The real threats, in order:
    55	
    56	1. **An agent doing something nobody asked for.**
    57	2. **Prompt injection** — poisoned text in a file, dependency, issue, or web page turns a
    58	   fooled-but-authorized agent into an attacker's hands. The load-bearing threat, because
    59	   the agent is *allowed* to read the repo.
    60	3. **Mishearing** — road noise, a passenger, a bad transcript.
    61	
    62	And now, promoted to first-class by round 4:
    63	
    64	4. **Malicious code that passes honest review** — threats 1 and 2 don't need to beat a
    65	   gate; they can walk a payload through the front door of a gate that's working
    66	   perfectly.
    67	
    68	---
    69	
    70	## The trusted computing base — named honestly
    71	
    72	The previous version claimed the release service was "the only trusted component." **False.**
    73	The things that must be trusted are:
    74	
    75	- The release service (holds the keys).
    76	- The larger harness, its web UI, model-response handling, the dispatch channel, evidence
    77	  capture, OpenClaw integration, deploy scripts, and the OS account they run under —
    78	  because they produce the facts the release service acts on.
    79	
    80	The design's job is to make this base **as small and as separated as possible**, not to
    81	pretend it's one component. Two separations do the heavy lifting:
    82	
    83	1. **The agents are outside the trusted base entirely** — different OS identity, isolated
    84	   storage, no keys (below).
    85	2. **The release service is separated FROM the rest of the harness** — it is the sole
    86	   writer of release facts; the harness submits authenticated observations through a narrow
    87	   interface and does not share the release account. Compromising the harness must not hand
    88	   over both the record and the release caller's identity.
    89	
    90	---
    91	
    92	## Where agents run — containment
    93	
    94	**DECIDED (Neill, 2026-07-15): a dedicated VM on this Mac** — Sol's stated minimum. The
    95	design stays hardware-agnostic, so migrating agent execution to a separate physical
    96	machine later is a config change, not a redesign. The mechanism:
    97	
    98	- **A dedicated virtual machine (minimum) or separate host (stronger)** runs all agent
    99	  execution. **No** owner-home sharing, **no** clipboard sharing, **no** credential
   100	  connections (SSH agent, git credential helper, cloud CLI config), **no** host-management
   101	  connection, **no** shared project history. The release service and production credentials
   102	  live OUTSIDE it.
   103	- **Isolated clones or storage snapshots, never shared working copies.** A standard git
   104	  worktree shares the underlying repo — history, branches, hooks, objects, settings. An
   105	  agent that can commit could corrupt shared history or damage other agents' branches, and
   106	  deleting its folder wouldn't undo it. Each agent gets a separate clone or a copy-on-write
   107	  snapshot with no path back to the real repo.
   108	- **A full containment profile, specified and tested**, covering every runner (Claude Code,
   109	  Codex, Ollama), every child process, every MCP server, every hook, every scheduled job,
   110	  every retry and recovery path — plus hard **resource limits**: CPU, memory, disk,
   111	  child-process count, execution time, network volume. An agent can brick the Mac by
   112	  exhaustion (fill the disk, fork-bomb, exhaust memory); a spend cap stops none of that.
   113	- **The kernel sandbox on, strict**: deny credential paths (`~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, keychain),
   114	  scrub credential env vars, narrow the domain allow-list (never a bare `github.com`
   115	  wildcard — domain fronting), `allowUnsandboxedCommands: false`. Noted honestly: a startup
   116	  check that asserts these is itself configuration; it detects drift, it is not independent
   117	  enforcement. The real boundary is the VM/host separation, with the sandbox as a second
   118	  layer inside it.
   119	
   120	---
   121	
   122	## No keys in agent hands — the broker
   123	
   124	Agents hold **no reusable credentials of any kind** — not GitHub write, not provider API
   125	keys, not subscription login material sitting in a readable file.
   126	
   127	- **A GitHub broker.** Agents do not push or open PRs. They hand a patch or a built package
   128	  to a broker; the broker creates the branch and the PR. GitHub protection covers branches,
   129	  tags, automation, packages, releases, production environments, and admin bypass — **not
   130	  just `main`**. (A token that can open a PR can usually also overwrite unprotected
   131	  branches, trigger paid automation, or run untrusted branch code in automation that holds
   132	  secrets. Narrow the authority to exactly "submit a patch.")
   133	- **A model/provider broker.** Agent runners get no reusable subscription or API
   134	  credentials; the broker holds them, applies per-job limits *before* every call, and is
   135	  the only path to a provider. This is required because a runner that can call its provider
   136	  directly can burn subscription limits or paid calls without ever reserving budget — so
   137	  "the release service holds all spend authority" is false unless provider access is
   138	  brokered too. Every metered provider also gets a hard provider-side cap (only a provider's
   139	  own cap is a real financial boundary; our record is a soft early warning).
   140	
   141	The honest note on runner authentication: a coding runner using a subscription login needs
   142	*some* auth material to exist. It is held by the broker, not placed in the agent
   143	environment — and this is exactly why agents live behind the VM/host boundary.
   144	
   145	---
   146	
   147	## The release service — the only irreversible power
   148	
   149	A small, separate service (a few hundred lines, the harshest-reviewed code in the system)
   150	holds every dangerous power: merge, deploy, spend, touch production. Concretely:
   151	
   152	- **Sole writer of release facts.** Verdicts, approvals, evidence provenance, and released
   153	  status are written only by the release service (or a release-account process), never by
   154	  the general harness directly. The harness submits authenticated observations through a
   155	  narrow interface.
   156	- **Not reachable by agents, and not a blind messenger.** No general network address agents
   157	  can reach; a local OS channel that checks the caller's actual account; no reusable
   158	  token in an agent-readable file; a fixed list of narrow actions with exact parameters
   159	  (repo, version, environment); single-use request numbers; approvals consumed atomically
   160	  before acting; rejection if `main`, the artifact, or the approval changes mid-operation.
   161	  Release requests come from a **deterministic controller that derives them from protected
   162	  state — never from model-written text.**
   163	- **Never runs untrusted code beside its credentials.** It must not build, install, test,
   164	  or execute agent-produced code in the environment that holds release credentials. This is
   165	  the single most dangerous mistake it could make.
   166	- **Re-verifies everything from its own records** before acting: the exact version passed
   167	  the full panel, no fail or unresolved escalation, a live single-use read-back approval
   168	  (with a tested recovery plan if irreversible) exists.
   169	
   170	### Evidence and reviews bound to one exact thing
   171	
   172	The database only proves observations were *recorded*. To mean anything, every review and
   173	artifact is bound to a single: request, source version, base version, **built package
   174	digest**, reviewer run, test run, and proposed release action. A real screenshot is
   175	worthless if it's from the wrong build, a stale process, a harmless screen while malice
   176	hides elsewhere, or a test that never exercised the dangerous path. "The file exists" (the
   177	round-3 hole) is necessary but nowhere near sufficient — the binding to the digest is what
   178	makes it evidence.
   179	
   180	### The package-based release chain
   181	
   182	Review and test a **clean package** built from the proposed version combined with current
   183	`main`, in the contained environment. Bind every verdict and artifact to that package's
   184	digest. **Deploy that exact package** — never a rebuilt approximation, never fresh
   185	untrusted build code next to release credentials.
   186	
   187	---
   188	
   189	## Untrusted-code policy — the round-4 crux
   190	
   191	Because model review can honestly pass malicious code, **agent-produced code is an
   192	untrusted payload even after it passes the gauntlet.** The design's response:
   193	
   194	- **Containment limits blast radius.** The deployed code inherits only the narrow
   195	  production privileges the release path grants — not the owner's full authority.
   196	- **A data-egress policy** (below) limits what a payload can exfiltrate even if it runs.
   197	- **Independent human review for high-authority change classes — DECIDED (Neill,
   198	  2026-07-15): ACCEPTED.** Changes that can gain production authority, alter dependencies,
   199	  change automation, handle secrets, change auth or money, modify stored data, or change
   200	  the release service itself **do not auto-release** — they wait for the owner's written
   201	  sign-off (with plain-English explanation of what the change does and why it's in a
   202	  dangerous class). Everything outside those classes ships autonomously through the
   203	  gauntlet. The release service enforces the classification: a change tagged
   204	  high-authority without a consumed human approval is refused, and classification is
   205	  derived by the deterministic release path from what the change touches — never from a
   206	  model's self-report.
   207	
   208	---
   209	
   210	## Data egress — protecting more than credentials
   211	
   212	The first design guarded credentials and ignored everything else worth protecting: private
   213	source, business plans, issue contents, conversation history, project memory, customer
   214	data, and **medical data in other projects**. An agent must be allowed to talk to its model
   215	provider — so a poisoned instruction can stuff private material into a *legitimate* model
   216	request, and a narrow domain allow-list doesn't help when the allowed destination is itself
   217	a channel that carries data out.
   218	
   219	So: an **enforced egress policy** defines what each project may send to each provider, and
   220	blocks customer/medical/private/cross-project data independently of credential scanning.
   221	**DECIDED (Neill, 2026-07-15): the PHI-bearing EMR is OUT of scope for this harness now,
   222	revisitable later.** The harness's project list simply does not include it, the agents'
   223	isolated environment contains no clone of it, and the egress policy still protects
   224	private source, business plans, and conversation data for the projects that are in scope.
   225	Any future decision to bring the EMR in requires the strict medical-data egress controls
   226	to be built and attack-tested first — that is a precondition, not a follow-up.
   227	
   228	---
   229	
   230	## The voice + Chief layer — deterministic control, not a chatty relay
   231	
   232	The voice stays a telephone, but round 4 made clear the control around it must be
   233	deterministic, not model-judgment:
   234	
   235	- **A deterministic turn controller** owns dispatch, with explicit states: listening →
   236	  complete-but-uncommitted → committed → superseded → dispatched → cancel-requested →
   237	  confirmed-stopped. **Only committed instructions may dispatch.** A model's guess that
   238	  speech finished is never enough for dangerous or costly work. This is what stops
   239	  "delete the— no, archive it" from dispatching the deletion on the partial.
   240	- **Exact delivery for anything that matters.** Warnings, numbers, consequences,
   241	  confirmations, and failure reports are delivered verbatim — never paraphrased by the
   242	  voice model, which can change meaning in both directions. (Verbatim *forwarding* of the
   243	  owner's speech is impossible — the mouth generates text from audio — so Chief confirms its
   244	  own read-back, and the confirmation attaches to that read-back, not to the mouth's guess.)
   245	- **A separate, hardwired emergency stop** that does not use Chief or any model, and is
   246	  reachable while driving without navigating a screen. A single tool that routes through
   247	  Chief means "stop everything" dies if Chief hangs.
   248	- **Authoritative acknowledgements only.** The 8-second "nothing started" message may only
   249	  be spoken after the turn is cancelled and the authoritative job record confirms no
   250	  dispatch exists — otherwise a network delay makes it a lie while work is actually running.
   251	  When unsure, say "I lost confirmation, use the emergency stop or check the written status."
   252	- **Structured state lives outside the conversation.** Job state, which instruction is
   253	  active, what was cancelled, which version was approved, whether an approval is live, and
   254	  which project is in scope come from structured records — never from Chief's rolled/
   255	  summarized history, which can drop a "not," revive a cancelled order, mix projects, or
   256	  carry an injection.
   257	- **Ambient speakers are an open risk stated plainly.** Phone auth proves which phone opened
   258	  the session, not who is talking. A passenger, radio, or podcast can utter a routine
   259	  dispatch or a stop. Voice is not strong enough to be an approval boundary; the mitigation
   260	  is that voice can never grant a dangerous action (that needs the written, stopped,
   261	  ideally biometric confirmation), and dangerous dispatch is gated regardless of who spoke.
   262	- **"Stopped and tapped" is a safety instruction to Neill, not a machine guarantee.** The
   263	  system cannot know the car is stopped. It is never stated as an enforced fact.
   264	- **Reconnection and long sessions** — session rollover, lost audio, phone calls,
   265	  navigation interruptions, network switches, and restoring exact conversation state after
   266	  a voice session expires — are part of the controller, with a realistic driving test set
   267	  (road noise, passengers, radio, weak signal, interruptions, self-corrections, ambiguous
   268	  confirmations) before the voice is trusted beyond calm, stopped conversation.
   269	
   270	Chief itself is `gpt-5.6-terra` (low effort, escalating to `gpt-5.6-sol` on pushback or a
   271	real decision), streaming, ~1.4s to first words. Terra-as-Chief isn't the risk; Chief
   272	*deciding routing and whether a correction supersedes* is, which is why the controls around
   273	it are deterministic.
   274	
   275	---
   276	
   277	## Resilience — backups, audit, incident response
   278	
   279	Round 4 restored items that fell out of the prior draft:
   280	
   281	- **Off-machine backups** of the safety record, job history, project memory, config, and
   282	  approval history — with **tested restoration drills**, not just backups that exist.
   283	- **Tamper-resistant audit records** stored off this Mac and off the trusted account, so a
   284	  compromise of either can't quietly rewrite history.
   285	- **Incident response**: emergency credential revocation, device-loss handling, compromised
   286	  phone-session handling, poisoned-memory recovery, and release-service compromise recovery.
   287	- **Crash recovery**: on startup, reconcile any `in_progress` job with a dead run id — never
   288	  silently lost — and ensure a stale approval can't remain live when unattended work
   289	  resumes.
   290	
   291	---
   292	
   293	## What "pinned" must actually cover
   294	
   295	Pinning OpenClaw alone is not enough — an upgrade can run data conversion under the trusted
   296	account before any startup check runs, and it doesn't pin the runner programs, MCP servers,
   297	plugins, model behavior, provider APIs, OS behavior, packages, or deploy tools. The pin
   298	list covers **all** of these, and every upgrade re-runs the full attack test (below) before
   299	it's trusted.
   300	
   301	---
   302	
   303	## Standing constraints (unchanged)
   304	
   305	- Text works fully without voice. Voice is a skin.
   306	- Tailscale only; nothing public.
   307	- Version-pin everything; deliberate upgrades only; never OpenClaw exec policy `full`/YOLO.
   308	- **Grok CLI does not touch the Arch EMR repo** until the repo-upload allegation is
   309	  independently disproven.
   310	
   311	---
   312	
   313	## The acceptance test
   314	
   315	**Before building:** Sol attacks this document and finds no path by which an agent can
   316	bypass the record, forge or misbind evidence, change any protected GitHub state, spend past
   317	a hard provider cap, reach production credentials, get untrusted code deployed with
   318	production authority *without the required independent review*, exfiltrate private/medical
   319	data, exhaust the host, or get a dangerous action performed without a live read-back
   320	approval bound to the exact package.
   321	
   322	**Before trusting the build:** the same list, run as a real attack test against every agent
   323	runner, every MCP server, every scheduled path, and on every upgrade of anything in the pin
   324	list. Anything that can do one of those stays disabled until the route is closed.
   325	
   326	---
   327	
   328	## ✅ OWNER DECISIONS — made by Neill, 2026-07-15
   329	
   330	These three reversed or constrained directions Neill set, and Sol made them conditions of
   331	approval. He decided all three:
   332	
   333	- **A. Human review for high-authority changes: ACCEPTED.** The dangerous change classes
   334	  (production authority, dependencies, automation, secrets, auth, money, stored data, the
   335	  release service itself) require his written sign-off before release; everything else
   336	  ships autonomously through the gauntlet. This resolves the crux of the round-4
   337	  rejection: honest model review can pass malicious code, so for the classes where that
   338	  matters most, a human is the backstop. Routine autonomy — the actual product goal — is
   339	  preserved for everything else.
   340	- **B. Where agents run: a dedicated VM on this Mac** (Sol's stated minimum). The design
   341	  is hardware-agnostic, so moving agent execution to a separate physical machine later is
   342	  a config change, not a redesign.
   343	- **C. The PHI-bearing EMR: out of scope now, revisitable later.** The harness's project
   344	  list does not include it and the agents' environment holds no clone of it. Bringing it
   345	  in later requires the strict medical-data egress controls to be built and attack-tested
   346	  first — a precondition, not a follow-up.

