# Verdict: NOT APPROVED

The document is materially better, but the deepest round-4 failure is not closed.

The main defect is simple: “qualified human review” has been replaced with “Neill’s written sign-off.” Neill can authorize business risk, but he cannot independently inspect hidden code. A plain-English explanation produced by the system under review is not independent technical review.

The second defect is just as serious: the document limits who may deploy code, but does not define what the deployed code may do once running. A tightly controlled delivery process can still deliver a payload that inherits broad access to production data, secrets, money, logs, and the internet.

## 1. The 12 required changes

I am using these meanings:

- “Genuinely addressed” means the design names a real boundary clearly enough to build and test.
- “Addressed only in words” means the requirement is repeated, but the enforcing mechanism is not defined.
- “Still missing” means the required protection was weakened, substituted, or omitted.

| # | Verdict | What carries the load, and what is wrong |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Dedicated VM or host | **Genuinely addressed as a choice; incomplete as a containment design** | The containment section says all agent execution occurs in a dedicated VM, with no owner-home, clipboard, credentials, host management, shared history, or release credentials ([containment section](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:98)). That satisfies the original architectural choice. It does not yet specify network separation, device sharing, fixed resource limits, virtual-disk behavior, or protection of the host and local network. |
| 2. Release service owns release facts | **Addressed only in words** | “Verdicts, approvals, evidence provenance, and released status are written only by the release service” carries the requirement ([release facts](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:152)). But the harness submits “authenticated observations.” If the whole harness shares one submitting identity, a compromised harness can submit false reviewer and test observations, and the release service will faithfully turn them into trusted facts. Sole writing does not establish truth. The phrase “or a release-account process” also opens an undefined second writer. |
| 3. Qualified human review for dangerous changes | **Still missing** | The required protection was “qualified human review.” The document instead says the change waits for “the owner’s written sign-off” based on a plain-English explanation ([human-review section](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:197)). Neill is not qualified to inspect code, and the explanation may be produced by the same compromised system. This is authorization, not independent technical review. The deterministic classification is also unsound, as explained below. |
| 4. Isolated clones, not shared working copies | **Genuinely addressed** | Separate clones or snapshots with no path to the real repository are explicitly required ([isolated storage](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:103)). This is the right boundary. It still needs proof during implementation. |
| 5. No direct GitHub write authority | **Addressed only in words** | The broker requirement and protection of branches, tags, automation, packages, releases, environments, and admin bypass are stated ([GitHub broker](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:127)). But “submit a patch” is not a native GitHub permission. The broker must hold wider authority and safely act on hostile input. The document does not define what patch forms it accepts, how it handles links, binaries and sub-project pointers, or how creation of a branch is prevented from running dangerous GitHub automation. |
| 6. Exact package-based release chain | **Addressed only in words** | The document says to build a clean package, bind every result to its fingerprint, and deploy that exact package ([package chain](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:180)). That is the correct goal. But the broker is also allowed to accept “a built package” from the agent. Those two statements conflict. The design does not identify a trusted builder, signed proof of origin, immutable package store, dependency-fetch rules, or final verification by the production target. |
| 7. Broker all provider access | **Addressed only in words** | The provider broker, per-job limit, and provider-side cap are stated ([provider broker](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:133)). The mechanism for subscription-based runners is not actually explained. Voice, Chief, reviewers, scheduled work, and remote tool servers are not explicitly required to use the same broker. Some providers do not offer a true hard spending stop. A billing alert is not one. |
| 8. Enforced data-egress policy | **Addressed only in words** | The document says an enforced policy blocks private and cross-project data ([egress section](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:219)). It does not say where enforcement occurs or how information is identified. There is also a basic contradiction: a remote coding model must receive private source code to work on it, while the policy says private source is blocked. The design must state which disclosure is deliberately permitted. An allowed model provider remains an exfiltration channel. |
| 9. Full containment profile | **Addressed only in words** | Every runner, child process, tool server, hook, schedule, retry, and resource limit is named ([containment profile](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:108)). But no actual profile, limits, network layout, or enforcement owner is specified. “Specified and tested” is a promise to write the missing design later. |
| 10. Deterministic voice controller | **Partly designed, but still only words as a safety boundary** | The state sequence, committed-turn rule, exact warnings, emergency stop, structured state, and reconnection tests are good requirements ([voice controller](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:235)). Missing are the event that makes speech “committed,” who may create that event, whether routine speech can be auto-committed, and exactly what the emergency stop kills. “Confirmed stopped” must mean every child process, remote job, retry and scheduled continuation is verified dead. |
| 11. Backups, audit and incident response | **Addressed only in words** | The document lists off-machine backups, restoration drills, off-account audit records, and response procedures ([resilience section](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:277)). It does not identify the independent storage, who controls it, how deletion is prevented, how secrets are rotated, or what successful restoration means. |
| 12. Rewrite the core claim | **Genuinely addressed as wording; not delivered by the design** | The weaker claim appears exactly as required ([core claim](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:40)). The rewrite is honest. The mechanisms underneath it are not yet sufficient. |

So: three are genuinely present at design level, eight are incomplete promises, and the most important one—qualified human review—has been replaced with something weaker.

## 2. Attack against the rewritten core claim

A concrete attack still succeeds:

1. An agent changes an ordinary application component. It adds a hidden trigger—for example, special behavior activated by a particular customer name, date, request header, or message.
2. The change does not touch a file the classifier recognizes as dangerous.
3. The model reviewers inspect it and honestly miss the trigger.
4. The tests exercise the normal behavior and pass.
5. A clean package is built. Its fingerprint is correct.
6. Every review and screenshot is genuinely bound to that package.
7. The release service deploys that exact package.
8. In production, the payload receives the application’s existing access: perhaps the customer database, stored credentials, logs, outgoing network connections, billing functions, or the ability to modify records.
9. The hidden trigger fires. The payload steals, corrupts or discloses information using authority the application legitimately needs.

Every checkpoint worked.

“Narrow deployment privileges” do not solve this. The permission to place a package in production is different from the authority the package receives after it starts. The document defines the former and barely defines the latter.

The egress promise does not close the attack. A payload can:

- Send information to a destination the application is already allowed to contact.
- Put private material inside a legitimate model request.
- Encode or divide information so a content scanner does not recognize it.
- Leak through logs, error reporting, DNS requests, customer-visible responses, timing, or usage measurements.
- Corrupt data without sending anything out.

The first half of the claim—“a skipped checkpoint cannot directly obtain release authority”—is also not established across the whole system. A GitHub automation job, remote tool server, scheduled task, old deployment script, or production platform could hold an independent route to deployment. The document says these must be covered, but does not require production to accept releases exclusively from the new release path.

## 3. The human-review boundary

Yes, an agent can get a high-authority change classified as routine.

The underlying mistake is classifying danger primarily from “what the change touches.” An ordinary application file can become dangerous whenever it runs with valuable production authority. A path-based classifier cannot determine intent.

Exact evasions include:

- **Build and installation behavior:** A change can hide commands in packaging instructions, container startup instructions, test setup, application startup, an ordinary helper program, or a script called by another script. Renaming the script defeats a list of known dangerous filenames.
- **Dependency records:** A lockfile alone can redirect a dependency to a hostile version, downloadable archive, private repository or package containing installation behavior. The main dependency declaration need not change.
- **Indirect dependencies:** A harmless-looking version update can replace a dependency several levels down. The visible requested package may remain trustworthy.
- **Generated code:** The agent can modify checked-in generated output rather than the generator. It can add a compiled, compressed or minified payload that reviewers cannot meaningfully inspect.
- **Generation inputs:** A harmless-looking schema, template or data file can cause dangerous executable output during the build.
- **Configuration that becomes behavior:** Routing rules, prompts, plug-in declarations, tool-server settings, scheduled-task definitions, templates, policy files, database statements and feature flags can all become executable behavior at runtime.
- **Ordinary source code:** Any normal application component can read secrets, alter records, make outgoing requests, or invoke privileged functions if the running application already has those powers.
- **Links and repository indirection:** A link, sub-project pointer, large-file pointer, rename, unusual capitalization or build-time download can make the reviewed path differ from the effective content.
- **Dormant behavior:** One routine submission adds a dormant loader. A later routine submission adds apparently harmless data that activates it.
- **Split authority:** One submission broadens what data can be read. Another adds an outgoing call. Neither appears catastrophic alone; together they exfiltrate the database.
- **Changes over time:** Five routine releases can cumulatively create high authority even if each individual difference looks small.
- **External activation:** The change can remain dormant until an existing production feature flag, customer input or scheduled event turns it on.

The classifier therefore cannot be “dangerous filenames versus routine filenames.”

It must start with the authority of the thing being changed:

- Any change to a service that can read private production data, write important records, use secrets, move money, administer users, or make broad outgoing connections is high-authority by default.
- Autonomous release is permissible only for components whose production access is already so narrow that malicious code cannot cause serious harm.
- Unknown file types, binaries, generated output, dependency changes, build behavior, configuration interpreted as code, links and sub-project pointers must fail into human review.
- Classification must consider the cumulative change since the last qualified human review, not only the latest submission.
- Changes to the classifier itself must always receive independent technical review.

That will reduce the amount of autonomous production release. That is the unavoidable consequence of accepting the round-4 finding.

## 4. Guarantees that remain ineffective

These claims still rest on records, configuration or good behavior:

- **“Independent human review.”** It is owner authorization based on an explanation, not technical inspection.
- **“Sole writer.”** It protects who writes the record, not whether the submitted observation is true.
- **“Deterministic classification.”** Deterministic mistakes remain mistakes. A file list cannot classify the authority of behavior.
- **“Containment limits blast radius.”** Production privileges are not actually enumerated.
- **“Data egress is blocked.”** No external enforcement point or workable information-labeling scheme is defined.
- **“No reusable credentials in runners.”** The subscription-runner mechanism is left unresolved, and remote tool servers may hold their own credentials.
- **“Provider-side hard caps.”** This is only true where a provider offers a genuine hard stop.
- **“Exact voice delivery.”** Critical text must bypass model paraphrasing entirely and go through fixed playback. Telling a model not to paraphrase is not enforcement.
- **“Emergency stop.”** A stop request is not a verified stop. Remote work, children, retries and schedules can survive it.
- **“Everything is pinned.”** Remote model behavior, provider services and operating-system behavior cannot literally be pinned. They can only be version-selected, monitored and re-tested.
- **“Tailscale only.”** Network privacy is not user authentication. A stolen or compromised tailnet device may still reach the service.
- **“Tamper-resistant audit.”** That depends entirely on the unstated storage and key arrangement.
- **“Tiny release service.”** The release service is given merge, deployment, spending and production authority in one place. That is a very large consequence from one compromise, even if the program itself is short.

## 5. The seams

These are the places where action may bypass the intended checkpoint:

- **Different agent runners:** Each runner may have its own login cache, update behavior, local helpers, shell startup, child processes, network settings and escape options.
- **Tool servers:** A local tool server may run outside the VM. A remote one may possess GitHub, email, cloud or database credentials. Its action occurs where that server runs, not where the agent runs.
- **Hooks and installation behavior:** Repository hooks, package installation hooks, test setup, build steps, container startup, editor helpers and operating-system login scripts can execute before the reviewed application starts.
- **Scheduled and recovered work:** Host schedules, VM schedules, GitHub schedules, cloud schedules, retries, queue redelivery and startup recovery can act without a fresh release request.
- **Voice, text and web:** The deterministic controller must govern every input door. A safe voice path is irrelevant if the text page, direct web request or old interface can dispatch directly.
- **The release service:** Hostile package names, archive contents, links, oversized requests and malformed records can attack the service without being “executed.” Its deployment tool may itself hold broad production authority.
- **The GitHub broker:** Creating a branch or PR can trigger automation before merge. Hostile patches can include links, binaries, sub-project pointers, automation changes or paths that escape the intended directory.
- **GitHub itself:** Administrator bypass, automation identities, package publishing, release creation, environment approvals and third-party GitHub applications may provide alternate authority.
- **The provider broker:** An allowed request can contain stolen information. Concurrent requests can outrun accounting. A remote provider response can contain a new poisoned instruction.
- **OpenClaw:** An upgrade can alter tool handling, stored state, permissions and data before the harness’s startup check runs.
- **Other upgrades:** Runner programs, tool servers, plug-ins, the VM, hypervisor, operating system, package manager, deployment platform and remote provider can each change behavior.
- **Production itself:** A cloud scheduler, administrator console, old deployment credential or existing production automation may deploy without the new release service.
- **Legacy code:** The current harness and older system must be removed from service, not merely ignored. A callable old route is a bypass.
- **Backup and restoration:** Restoring old configuration can revive old credentials, stale approvals, unsafe automation or a vulnerable release path.

Production must reject anything not signed by the sole approved chain. Merely saying “all releases should use this service” is not enough.

## 6. The dedicated VM on the business Mac

The listed separation is necessary but not sufficient.

A hostile guest can still affect the Mac through:

- CPU, memory, heat, battery, storage and network exhaustion.
- A dynamically expanding virtual disk filling the host.
- Access to the Mac, local network, tailnet, router, printers, storage devices or other business machines through ordinary networking.
- Shared folders other than the owner’s home, drag-and-drop, USB, microphone, camera, Bluetooth, printers and virtual-device connections.
- VM snapshots, crash dumps and backups retaining credentials or private source.
- Bugs in the VM software that allow escape into the Mac.
- Social engineering that persuades Neill to paste a secret, mount a folder or approve a system dialog.
- Denial of the release service or brokers running on the same physical machine.
- A broker being tricked into acting for the guest even though the guest cannot contact the release service directly.

For a same-Mac VM, the design must require:

- No shared folders at all, no drag-and-drop and no device passthrough.
- Fixed—not expanding—disk limits, plus hard CPU, memory, process and network limits.
- No direct access to the host, local network, tailnet or nearby devices.
- Outgoing access only through explicitly named brokers.
- No incoming connection to the VM.
- No Tailscale identity inside the VM.
- A host firewall that remains effective even if guest configuration changes.
- Prompt security updates for the Mac and VM software.
- A tested response to disk filling, memory pressure, network flooding and VM crashes.

Even then, the VM and the business remain in one physical failure domain. A separate machine is materially safer. The owner may accept the residual risk of a VM, but the document must state that this protects mainly against ordinary containment failures; it does not make hostile execution on the personal Mac risk-free.

## 7. Missing entirely

The document lacks complete designs for:

1. **A qualified technical reviewer.** Who can inspect dangerous code independently, and how their approval is tied to the exact package.
2. **Production authority per application.** Exactly which data, secrets, network destinations and actions each deployed component receives.
3. **Authenticated written approval.** How the system proves Neill—not a stolen session or injected web request—signed, preferably using a device-bound passkey or biometric confirmation.
4. **A fail-closed classification policy.** Including cumulative changes, generated code, binaries, dependencies, build behavior, configuration, links and unknown content.
5. **Safe patch intake.** The exact format the broker accepts and how hostile files, paths, links and repository indirection are rejected.
6. **A trusted clean builder.** Including dependency access, reproducibility, signed proof of origin and an immutable package store.
7. **Production verification.** The production target itself must verify the signed package fingerprint before starting it.
8. **Safe rollout and rollback.** Limited first deployment, health checks, automatic halt, data backup before destructive changes, and rollback that cannot revive an unsafe package.
9. **Detection after release.** Independent monitoring for unusual database access, outgoing traffic, money movement, secret access and unexpected background activity.
10. **A kill mechanism for deployed payloads.** The voice emergency stop addresses jobs, not malicious code already running in production.
11. **Web and API authentication.** Tailscale alone is not enough.
12. **Separation of dangerous powers.** Merge, package signing, deployment, spending and direct production access should not all belong to one small service.
13. **Secret lifecycle.** Creation, limited delivery, rotation, expiration, emergency revocation and proof that old copies are gone.
14. **A realistic update policy.** Security updates cannot be forbidden indefinitely, while remote model and provider behavior cannot be perfectly pinned.

## Existing system check

The statement “almost nothing here is built” is accurate.

The existing system still:

- Launches model runners directly ([current dispatch path](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:129)).
- Lets the general harness write reviewer results ([current review recording](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:257)).
- Allows autonomous shipping through the old record rules ([current shipping path](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:173)).
- Holds a provider key in the web service and mints voice access directly ([current voice access](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/server.py:298)).
- Uses model-based speech completion ([current voice turn detection](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/server.py:332)).
- Treats capability rows as if they were physical restrictions, although they are records describing permission ([current capability records](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:269)).

Those old paths must be disabled before any new boundary is credited. Leaving them available “for compatibility” would invalidate the new architecture.

## 8. Required for approval

### Must change the design

Ranked most important first:

1. **Restore the actual human-review requirement.** High-authority changes require review by a qualified, independent technical human. Neill separately authorizes the business decision. If no qualified reviewer is available, those changes do not release.
2. **Classify by production authority, not filenames.** Any change to a component with valuable production access is dangerous by default. Autonomous release is limited to components whose runtime authority is already harmless. Classification covers cumulative changes since the last human review.
3. **Define production containment.** State exactly what every deployed component can read, write, spend, contact and reveal. This is the missing boundary against code that passes review.
4. **Make the release path truly exclusive.** Production accepts only packages signed through this chain. Disable all old deployment scripts, automation identities, administrator bypasses and alternate routes.
5. **Define the trusted package chain completely.** The agent submits only a restricted patch—not a built package. A clean builder creates the package, records signed origin, stores it immutably, and production verifies the signature and fingerprint.
6. **Replace the egress slogan with enforceable architecture.** Put network enforcement outside the agent and production workloads. Define which project information may deliberately be sent to which provider. Admit that an allowed provider can receive allowed private source; do not claim that generic scanning can prevent all encoded exfiltration.
7. **Define every broker’s exact authority and hostile-input rules.** Include GitHub automation, remote tool servers, voice, Chief, reviewers and scheduled work.
8. **Complete the VM profile.** No host or local-network reachability, no shared devices, fixed resources, broker-only outgoing traffic, and an explicit statement of same-Mac residual risk.
9. **Define authenticated human approval and exact critical-message delivery.** Critical warnings are played from fixed text, not paraphrased. Written approval is device-bound, short-lived, single-use and tied to the exact package and action.
10. **Separate dangerous powers.** The release component should not also hold general spending and production-data authority. Give each action the smallest separate identity possible.
11. **Specify rollout, monitoring, kill and rollback controls.**
12. **Rewrite the acceptance test.** “An attacker finds no path” is not a provable test. Use explicit attack cases, independent inspection, stated residual risks and fail-closed results.

### Must be proven during the build

- Every runner, child process, tool server, hook, schedule, retry and recovery path stays inside the intended boundary.
- No reusable credential is readable in the VM.
- The VM cannot reach the host, tailnet, local network, production or release service.
- Resource attacks cannot freeze or fill the business Mac.
- GitHub branch creation and pull requests cannot run automation with valuable credentials.
- The harness cannot invent a reviewer identity or submit a result on another reviewer’s behalf.
- The exact reviewed package is the exact package production starts.
- All classifier evasions named above are rejected or escalated.
- Spending stops at a real provider boundary under concurrent requests.
- Private cross-project material cannot enter an allowed provider request.
- Partial speech, reconnection, interruption and session rollover cannot dispatch an uncommitted order.
- Emergency stop proves that all local and remote continuations are dead.
- Backups restore successfully, old credentials are revoked, and audit history survives compromise of the Mac.
- Every current direct-write, direct-runner, direct-provider and old shipping path is disabled.
- Each upgrade repeats these attacks before being trusted.

The design is not ready to build. The owner decisions are recorded, but decision A was not implemented as required: written owner consent is not qualified independent review. Until that and production runtime containment are corrected, the round-4 attack remains open.
